"Legion starts each new issue in its project's order, once its blockers clear and a place under the project's cap is free. A place under the cap holds one issue with its owner, whatever work units, steps or workers it runs, and returning it stops no agent."
"Legion's daemon moves work by recorded rules, which give the same result for the same records, events, service answers and due timers. The daemon does not decide what a review finding or a failed check means for the work, and follows the recorded outcome."
"Legion's daemon keeps a copy of the status, blockers, parent and claim of each Dispatch issue that it reads. It replaces a copied value only with a reading at a newer Dispatch sequence."
"Legion runs each work unit through the steps of the workflow version that the unit's owner chose or wrote. A new workflow version needs no change of Legion's code, and neither adds nor removes an approval that the person must give."
"Legion's daemon builds each issue's state from Dispatch issue events and from GitHub pull request, review, push and check events that Envoy publishes. It reads GitHub only in reply to an event, at a handoff, and once for each issue at its own start."
"Legion starts no phase of an issue tree, and merges none of its changes, while an approval that the tree waits on does not stand. Until its repository holds The Spec, each top-level Legion issue waits for the person's approval of its spec."
"The role prompts and skills that Legion and Envoy ship name no deployment, project, private repository or person, and quote no measurement or incident. Legion adds the deployment's own instructions file after its role prompts, in the prompt of every role."
"In any minute, Legion starts, resumes or wakes no more agent sessions than its configured rate plus burst, by default 8 plus 1. It delays each wake past that and drops none, and a rate of 0 turns the pacing off."
"Legion gives each agent session that it starts an environment with the tools, access and resources that the operator provides. Work on one issue changes no working file of another issue."
"Legion's merger role, not its daemon, merges each pull request, as Legion's implementing GitHub App and only at the head it read. On a branch with a merge queue it puts that head in the queue, and it never turns on automatic merge."
"Legion's daemon moves an approved issue from review to acceptance only when GitHub's merge state for the approved head is clean, unstable or has hooks. It reads no ruleset and no list of required checks."
"Legion checks the reviewer's approval against the current head before acceptance, and not again when the merger starts. The architect's sign-off and an open design gate also hold the merger back."
"Legion's daemon takes its project, service addresses, credentials, runtime, worker image and model overlays from its deployment's settings file. Its code names no host, repository, account or person of one deployment."
"Through Legion's daemon, the project controller can set an issue's status in Dispatch to todo, backlog or icebox, and to no other status. The daemon refuses that change from any session that is not the project's controller."
"Legion's daemon answers each worker plugin in the shape of the contract number that the plugin's build states. A plugin refuses an answer that it cannot read, and names both contract numbers and each wrong field."
"Where settings turn writing review on, it checks the agent texts that they cover, and holds a text only where they require a hold. A rewritten text keeps its original behind a badge, and each person sees daily counts of texts held, rewritten, passed, unreviewed or posted despite objections."
"Dispatch lets a person arrange and see work by project, priority, state and time. A priority is a ranked goal with an owner that work of any project can advance, and it is not an issue."
"Dispatch draws each kind of record the same way in every list, and each reference to a record the same way on every page. Its lists, filters and text fields share one component for each kind of record, value or purpose, except document bodies and live agent conversations."
"Dispatch shows an issue's conversation, spec, verification, plan and decisions on the issue's own page. From that page, a person reaches each document of the issue and its history."
"Dispatch stores issues, documents, conversations and decisions as linked records with named relations. Each decision links to its author, and to the question that it settles where there is one."
"Each ask, approval request, credential request and thread that waits on a person reaches that person's one Inbox. It stays there until someone settles, dismisses or hands it on, and that person's answer reaches the agent that asked."
"An edit changes only what it names, its event, the questions that it opens or closes, and the approval requests that its new version moves. It never drops another writer's concurrent edit, and earlier versions stay readable unless a permitted deletion hides their text."
"Each read or change of a record that Dispatch's pages offer is also available as a request or a terminal command, under the same permissions."
"Dispatch's server takes its database, sign-in provider, GitHub App, embedding and answer models and message hub from its deployment's settings. An optional setting left unset turns its feature off or takes one stated default."
"While two builds of Dispatch serve during a release, each accepts the other's requests, stored documents, bus messages and subscription settings. A page that the earlier build served keeps working."
"Dispatch refuses an edit that would grow a document past 1 MiB of text or 65,536 elements, and names the bound and the size. The editor shows the reason and drops only the refused edit."
"Dispatch refuses a comment, comment edit, message, broadcast or reply that holds a block over 32,000 text units, or more than 32,768 blocks. The refusal names the field, the bound and the count over it."
"When a person approves, Dispatch records the person, the time, the approval request that it answers, the version approved and a hash of its text. It refuses a person's approval from a credential without the grant to approve."
"Dispatch holds the approved Spec as nodes: statements in one tree, their checks, and the components and interfaces they are about. Only the person's approval of a change request adds, changes or removes a statement, component or interface in it."
"When someone writes a new issue or question, Dispatch offers the nearest existing issues and answered questions, up to a stated number. It says when it could not search, and that is different from no match."
"Dispatch shows recorded measures of how long work takes, what it costs and how often changes fail, each with its method. A missing measure never shows as zero."
"Each page of Dispatch works with the keyboard alone and on a phone. Content that others add does not move a reader's place in older content while that content and its version stay on screen."
"Dispatch's server publishes one definition of its routes, and its web app and command line take each request and answer from it. A client that does not follow a changed route fails to build."
"An approval in Dispatch stands for the version it names until a changes request or a new version. A new version keeps it only when its text is the approved text, apart from the approver's own later answers."
"Envoy records whether each session is live, unresponsive or gone, and which process holds each role. A lookup or a delivery attempt never releases a role, and a release frees only the claim of the process that holds it."
"Envoy keeps and redelivers a message accepted for a session until the session saves it in its conversation, or a stated limit removes it. Envoy tells a session when a message that it did not get is gone."
"Envoy's listener and secrets broker take each host name, service account and machine that they trust from their deployment's settings. Envoy's code, tests and examples name no host, cloud project or account of one deployment."
"Envoy's listener decides each grant, renewal, release and delivery of a role from that role's claim record, read when it acts. When the read fails, the act waits and changes nothing."
"Envoy takes GitHub, Slack and Ghost Wispr events at its webhooks, checks the signature of each, and publishes the supported ones as messages. It folds a commit's check runs into one record, and publishes a message when they settle."
"Envoy's secrets broker allows the use of a credential under the rule of that secret: its scope, its duration and its approval. The rule of a secret says whether the broker releases its value, mints a short-lived token, or makes the call itself."
"A project can try an agent setting, such as memory, by drawing a variant at random for each new issue or each new issue tree. Legion records the variant that each issue or issue tree drew, so that a reader can compare the outcomes of variants."
"Legion and Envoy record their failures with the cause, and alert on each condition that they declare. A health answer of Dispatch or Envoy says whether that service can do its work now."
"A merged change to the three systems reaches production by itself, unless a recorded hold, a failed safety check or a required approval stops it. During a release, no request goes unanswered: it succeeds or its caller gets an error."
"Each work unit that Legion runs has one owner on record, the agent that answers for it. After an unexpected stop, Legion resumes the agent with any task still owed, in its saved conversation where it exists, within its restart budget."
"From the records of Dispatch, a person can get an answer that names its sources, and can do an operation by a request in words. Dispatch's server calls the models and runs their tools itself, needs no running agent session, and records each such operation."
"Legion lands a change only when the checks of each statement that it builds or restores pass on the commit that lands. Each behavior that it adds or fixes has a test that fails without the change."
"Each Dispatch act, Envoy message, broker call and Legion commit names its caller, and a Dispatch act names the person an agent acted for. Each system refuses a read or a write that the caller has no permission for."
"Each result of a check names the check, the run that produced it, the change that it tested and what it found. A result keeps its verdict, Dispatch shows separately whether it holds for the change in question, and a check that did not run never passes."
"From Dispatch, a person can read a running agent session's conversation and send it a message or a command. Where a session cannot take one of these, Dispatch says so."
"For each merged commit, each merge-queue check that its files can affect runs on code containing it, before the merge or on main after. Each run selects its checks by its event, and a change's run runs a passing check once in each configuration that can change its result."
"The main branch of the three systems' repository takes a change only from its merge queue, as a squashed pull request with one approving review. GitHub merges it only when the checks ci-ok and pr-title pass and the change adds no CodeQL alert of high severity or more."
"Legion writes an issue's status in Dispatch only at the Dispatch sequence that it last read. Dispatch refuses the write when the issue changed after that read."
"Dispatch appends each change of an issue, document or project to its event log, and a publisher sends it without a timed search. One publisher holds the work, a standby takes over within six seconds, and each destination keeps one record's order."
"Dispatch learns of a GitHub change from the events that Envoy publishes. Where an event never arrives, its own timers bring the record up to date: a sweep after two minutes, and reads after five minutes."
"The Spec names one writer component for each kind of record or shared value that its statements name. It names each other component that holds that record as a copy."
"Each statement of The Spec that states a bound also states what its component does at that bound. Each such statement has a check that runs the component at that bound."
"Each interface that carries changes of records between components has a statement that names the events it carries, or the timer of its reads. A statement of a timer gives its period."
"Each interface of The Spec has a statement that names the declared contract of what its sides exchange. That statement has a check that compares each side with the contract."
"Each statement, component and interface of the approved Spec has a record of the person's approval that covers its current text. Each check has one too, or a check review that found it no weaker."
"It runs agents to turn approved work into software changes."
"It admits issues, shares the capacity of a project between issues, and keeps ownership and dependencies consistent."
"It decides which steps of a work unit can run, from the unit's workflow version, what the unit waits on and the recorded outcomes."
"It starts, resumes and observes agents with their assigned roles."
"It provides isolated computing and repository workspaces for agent work."
"It publishes the change of a work unit for review and brings it into the repository under the rules of that repository."
"It applies the runtimes, models, role instructions and limits that a deployment chooses, and keeps the experiment group of each issue or issue tree."
"It is the one agent of a project that ranks waiting work and settles what the owners of work cannot agree on."
"It lets an agent in its session take its assignment, report its state and its completion, and get a reminder of open work."
"It holds work, documents and decisions for people and their agents."
"It holds each record of Dispatch and the relations between records, and it resolves each reference to a record."
"It lets people and agents write and read versioned text and attachments."
"It holds the threads of comments, messages and replies between people and agents, with their authors and their history."
"It records questions, answers, decisions and what the person approved."
"It shows a person what waits on that person, and keeps what that person read, answered or dismissed."
"It finds relevant records and answers or acts on a request of a person."
"It connects statements to checks and records what each check run found."
"It computes and shows work duration, delivery health, and each other measure that a project registers."
"It decides what each person, agent or service can read or change."
"It reviews what an agent writes to a person before Dispatch takes it, and returns advice or a refusal."
"It lists agent sessions and shows one session while it runs, where a person can steer it."
"It gives each part of Dispatch one way to navigate and to lay out a page, by keyboard and on a phone."
"It gives each action of Dispatch as a command in a terminal, for an agent or a person."
"It connects agent sessions to messages, outside events and controlled credentials."
"It stores and delivers messages until the receiving session records them."
"It records session presence, roles and the session that holds each role."
"It turns events from outside services into messages that the systems can act on."
"It authorizes credential use and forwards requests that need protected secrets."
"It lets an agent session register its presence, and receive and send messages through Envoy."
"It tests each change before or after it merges, and releases and deploys what merged."
"It runs a model with tools in one way for each part of the three systems that needs a model."
"It exchanges work, approvals, execution state and results between the two systems."
"It carries workflow notices and session presence between the two systems."
"Dispatch publishes each change of a record and chooses who gets it, and Envoy delivers it to sessions."
"The web app, the command line and other callers use one described set of requests."
"An agent in its session and Legion agree on compatible messages and on the work that a report is about."
"It keeps presence, role ownership and delivery consistent when a session loses its connection for a short time."
"It brings outside changes into work state and delivery measurements."
"It tells Legion which approval of which document an issue waits on, and whether that approval stands."
"It ties an approval to the exact text that the person approved, and shows when that text changed after."
"Dispatch lists a credential request and links to the page of the broker, where the person decides."
"It carries authenticated caller identity and permissions across service boundaries."
"It makes each result of a check available in Dispatch with its source and its commit, from an agent or from CI."
"Each recorded copy of a fact states the time, or the owner's version, at which it was read from its owner."
"A limit, schema, list or value that several parts of the three systems use is defined in one place. Every other part reads that definition or is generated from it."
"One place in the code answers each question that several acts depend on, such as whether a decision of the person is still current. Where no single place is possible, a check finds every site that must agree."
"Each fact has one owning system, which writes its value of record, and any other system changes that value only through the owner. Every other record of the fact is a copy: the copy follows the owner, and the owner never follows a copy."
"An act on a role or a hold reads that fact from its owner as it acts, and a role act waits while it cannot. A message about a piece of work decides nothing; an agent's session reads the state of its work from that state's owner."
"A system never presents an incomplete copy of its owner's records as a complete reading, and an entry missing from it never reads as absent. A refill removes an entry only after reading every record of the owner."
"A copy takes each newer value from its owner, and a slower write, an older read or a failed read never replaces a newer value. A system that decides from a copy uses the newest owner value that it holds for that fact."
"Dispatch serves each stored text, measure and per-issue value from one source, and every page and command that shows it reads that source. Its pages search records through the server, not a store of their own."
"A record takes the time, commit or session that it names from the system that made it, such as the server's clock or Git history. A time or commit typed into the text of a record is never read as that value."
"The person's settings choose coverage, rules, models, limits, holds and what posts when the writer does not act, and review imposes no other holds. When enabled, Dispatch's server checks each covered agent text against review rules and its reader's recorded questions about words, regardless of client or form."
"Dispatch counts text with a recorded session actor or a person's credential acting for a session as agent text, except the Legion daemon's own session. People's own text and agent-only messages bypass review, while handoffs, results, approval requests, files and plugin-generated rather than agent-written text post immediately without holds."
"Dispatch hides agent writing of kinds the settings hold from people's reads, counts, feeds and notices until it posts, except question blocks within saved documents. Dispatch saves document versions immediately, while held question blocks remain visible there but absent from the person's Inbox, counts and notices until publication."
"When enabled, the plugin's check refuses standalone and document questions recommending no change or requesting a person's action before the agent tries its access. While enabled, it opens any refused question on a reasoned resubmission or third submission, and an access refusal on the attempted operation and error."
"Draft-only review returns findings, a rewrite and each call's model deployment without changing records, while held-text review sends findings and a rewrite to the writer. If the writer is not running or misses the configured wait, Dispatch immediately posts the original or marked rewrite, as the settings choose."
"When its setting is on, the reminder refuses once a session's first write of a kind review may hold and shows the writing rules. It repeats once after each replacement of the session's conversation with a shorter account, independently of whether the reviewer is on."
"The plugin's separate question check stays until the person's answer to the measured results chooses its removal. Until that answer, the server reviewer and the writing reminder are off."
"For each day, project and person, Dispatch shows the person counts of texts held, rewritten, passed, posted unreviewed and posted despite findings. Dispatch records reasons for posting over findings and posts the original when review fails or times out."
"Dispatch keeps each rewritten text's original wording, who rewrote it and why. Each rewritten text shows a short badge, its only rewrite notice, that lets the person switch between its rewritten wording and its original."
"Legion lets a project declare experiments, the memory setting among them, each with a mode, a unit of tree or issue, and named variants. A variant that adds no setting is the control, and work in it runs as work outside any experiment runs."
"A project sets each experiment off, on or random: off gives new work no variant, and on gives each new unit one named variant. A random experiment draws one variant for each new unit, when Legion first admits it, from a cryptographic random source."
"Legion starts each agent of a piece of work with the settings of its drawn variants that are still declared, and of no other. A variant no longer declared adds nothing, and the work keeps its record of the draw."
"A measure records the outcome of each piece of work in an experiment once that work has ended. A reader can link each such outcome to that work and to the variant that it drew."
"Legion's documentation gives a template of what an experiment states before it starts. It names the primary outcome, the unit, the smallest effect worth acting on, the units needed and the stop date."
"A web page lets people explore what a memory holds for Legion's agents."
"Legion records the variant of each unit in each experiment once, with the mode and the time of the draw. Re-admission and a new generation never rewrite that record."
"Work that Legion first admitted before an experiment existed holds no variant of it, runs none of its variants, and is never drawn later."
"Legion shows each issue's variant in each experiment, with the mode and the time of the draw, by request and in a terminal. Its view of settings shows each declared experiment."
"Each service of Legion and Envoy writes its records as named fields that the log store reads, at a level stating how serious each is. A standing condition writes a record when it starts and when it ends."
"When Envoy cannot read a source in full, its answer says so. It never serves a partial or missing reading as a complete one, or as zero."
"For each alert condition that Legion or Envoy declares, or that the person's decision requires, a watcher opens its alert whenever that condition holds. Each declaration states its treatment of missing data, its readers and their first action."
"A delivered alert carries its summary, its observed value and its documentation, and a close only where it asks for one. An alert that reaches no reader stays unacknowledged, and a path independent of its delivery reports that failure."
"Where another session owns or controls stalled work or a call past its bound, Legion tells it once, with evidence; designed waits are not stalls. It reports a stopped agent only after recovery fails, and tells the person of each failure that reaches no agent able to act on it."
"Legion records each failure of work with a cause from a fixed set, its time and its attempt counts. Its failure notice carries those counts with their limits, and the operator's view also serves each failed send's count, time and error."
"Legion records each act of its daemon, and what each waiting piece of work waits on and how old that reading is. From these records, a reader tells stopped work from moving work without an agent's transcript."
"A health answer of Dispatch or Envoy says whether that service can do its work now, and names each reason that it cannot. When the Envoy listener's own check finds its delivery stopped, it records that and repairs itself or restarts."
"Legion runs each piece of work only under a workflow version its owner chose or wrote; each work unit names the version it runs. Changing its steps, order, roles, start conditions, completion checks, outcome paths, attempt limits or worker counts needs no change to Legion's code."
"Legion schedules work units, including ones added during execution, by their workflow version's order, start conditions, checks and outcome paths. Approvals required for the work still hold each step; independent steps run together as its version permits, without a daemon-imposed worker limit."
"Each workflow version records every step's role, start condition, completion checks, failure path, attempt limit and path at that limit, with role instructions and model. Legion runs agent steps with their recorded role's instructions and model, follows each step's paths, and stops on failure or exhausted attempts without destinations."
"Every workflow version Legion runs leaves approvals of the work to The Spec; it neither adds one nor skips a required one. Legion applies those work approvals whatever workflow version runs."
"Legion records each workflow step's run as a result naming its step, workflow version and every work unit it ran on. The result records its outcome and the identity that ran it."
"Legion blocks a step runner's normal exit until it writes its result, unless that runner's start record was refused. A runner whose start record is refused stops without doing work."
"Legion runs all sessions of one issue in one pod, made from the same definition as a person's agent pod. The sessions share its working copy, disk and limits, and each keeps its own home, credentials and processes."
"Each issue has its own working copy, which holds no work of another issue. Work on one issue changes no working file of another issue, in a pod or on a shared build service."
"Each new issue pod starts from the issue's pushed branch plus the newest copy of its unpushed work, and its working copy stays usable. Legion's daemon copies that work while the pod runs and keeps it whichever way the pod ends, and no pod replacement waits for a push."
"Each session that Legion starts can run each tool that its work needs where that work runs, from its image or added later. It starts with the repository's packages installed and its deployment's plugins, skills, instructions and settings, and changed ones reach later pods without a new image."
"Each project's deployment declares in code its sessions' account, permissions and direct network routes, reaching every service and store that the project's work needs. A person or a merge applies each change, and a deployment can keep one project's data out of other projects' sessions and Legion's logs."
"Each session that Legion starts has the processor, memory and disk that its work needs, in its pod or on build services. Its deployment sets the amounts, and when a pod runs out of memory, one process ends and the pod's other processes go on."
"A run larger than its issue pod runs on build services that its deployment selects, which hold the run and grow with the load. The issue pod can start such a run and read its inputs, results and logs."
"Legion gives each session the credentials that its project's deployment grants, and it narrows none of them. It keeps each such credential valid while the session runs."
"A session's process runs no turn until it has Legion's plugin and has registered its work, and otherwise ends naming the cause. When its checkout, a package install or its test database fails, Legion tells the session the cause and records which listed capabilities the session lacks."
"The broker authenticates each requesting machine and session, and keeps their person, service and project permissions distinct. Machines sharing a fleet identity cannot end or take over each other's access."
"The broker grants a credential request exactly when the secret's rules for identity, scope, duration and approval permit it, and serves nothing beyond them. It asks a person only when required, reuses live grants, and stops grants at expiry, revocation or session end."
"The broker accepts a person's secret decision only through its own page and sign-in, after checking that person's permission. Dispatch's deployment cannot decide a secret request, and the broker refuses requests with conclusive failures before asking a person."
"Where its rules permit a model decision, the broker's judge grants only one secret, for at most 15 minutes. The person sees and can end each judged grant, and a judge failure leaves the request for that person."
"The broker follows the secret's rule for each granted request: release its stored value, mint and return a short-lived token, or make the call. Only a reported failure stops that service, and the broker never releases a value whose rule says to mint from it or call with it."
"For secrets confined to broker calls, the broker sends credentials only to the hosts, headers, methods and paths that their rules allow. It refuses private addresses, gives requesters neither these values nor their minted tokens, and leaves other request fields unchanged."
"A protected secret is the broker's own credential, a value it mints from, or a value its rule keeps in calls. The broker sends each only where minting or an allowed call needs it, never in messages, records, replies or logs."
"The broker validates credentials against their kinds before use, refuses invalid writes, and records removal of trailing line endings without secret values. It removes the trailing line endings of a stored value when the rest is nonempty and holds no line ending, and otherwise keeps every byte."
"The broker explains and records each refusal without secret values, and retries temporary mint failures within the caller's deadline. It preserves grant checks for requests still in progress during shutdown, and live grants during client upgrades."
"The code and shared instructions of Legion, Dispatch and Envoy contain no policy or facts specific to a deployment, project or person. Legion's skills contain no specific numbers, token counts, token limits, times, dates, project names or incident lists."
"Each deployment can choose the systems' models and providers, and Legion's agent software and fallback order; each choice it leaves unset has one shared value. Legion requires no particular provider or gateway, builds its Claude Code support in full, and its handling of provider refusals covers every supported provider."
"A Legion deployment can add tools and supply roles and instructions, which override Legion's optional default roles; Legion gives guidance for writing roles and skills. Any agent role or instruction of the three systems that a deployment does not set has one value shared by every deployment."
"The systems use each deployment's host addresses, machines, worker software, networking and outside services, and support separate production and test deployments. Envoy works the same on each supported cloud; Dispatch and Legion neither create nor manage machines, and no system requires a person's computer."
"Each deployment can set the systems' resource settings and work limits; any limit it leaves unset has one stated value shared by every deployment. Legion sets no cap on concurrent workers inside a pod, and applies without restarting the daemon settings that the person changes in Dispatch."
"Each deployment supplies accounts, credentials and permissions to Legion, Dispatch and Envoy, and chooses which sign-in methods each enables. Legion's daemon adds no worker-access restriction beyond deployment grants; Legion and Dispatch accept any permitted issue repository regardless of project."
"Each deployment chooses whether its projects use memory or assign it at random. Legion passes the deployment's memory settings to workers without writing its own."
"Legion has no built-in machinery for heavy jobs, machine types, merge scripts, recurring issues, chat subscriptions, fork rules, standing leads or campaign coordinators. Legion holds no built-in rule for which issues a controller admits."
"Deployments choose Legion's prompts, admission rules, checkout behavior and status-request intervals; Dispatch accepts multiple architecture sources per project. One source describes Legion's settings, their defaults and required environments; deployments choose whether paused Legion sessions answer messages."
"A deployment's repository declares each of its production resources in code, and the jobs and data work that a release needs run by themselves. The repository records each step of a release still done by hand, with the issue that removes it or what blocks it."
"Unless a recorded hold stops it, a merge to main of the three systems' code publishes its build and packages by itself. A program moves each unheld pin of those images, and images built on them, to a verified build carrying the merge, unless verification refuses it."
"A hold stops one group of pins from moving, and it records its reason, who set it and any issue that must be done first. A hold older than seven days, or whose issue is done, raises an alert."
"Where CI releases a change, only a recorded hold, a failed safety check or an approval that the person requires stops that release. The person requires one only for a new application or a change to root or seed credentials, and each stop names its reason."
"After each release, an automated check confirms that production runs a build that carries the merge, and it asks no person to look. A merge or build not live 60 minutes after the rules first let CI move it raises one alert naming its stage and owner."
"A release replaces the pods of each service one at a time, and a service with two or more pods keeps answering throughout. During a release, each request succeeds or its caller gets an error."
"A release of Legion reaches its running pods through the daemon's paced update rounds, with no stop of Legion. Its parts move in an order in which nothing is used before it exists."
"Each change that lands on main of a deployment's repository leads to an apply of each program that it changed. CI applies each program of the three systems by itself, as that program's own identity; a hand-started run applies only the programs it names."
"For each merged commit, its files' affected merge-queue checks run on code containing it, before the merge or on main afterwards. The queue selects every affected check, including slow suites but excluding checks that run only on pull requests."
"With readable inputs, pull-request and merge-queue runs start only checks affected by changed files, except title validation; named-file runs add no unrelated checks. An event-declared check runs when an input it reads changes; each assertion has one check at the lowest layer that proves it."
"Each run selects checks by its event; title, body and review changes run only title validation, while comments start no tests. Merge-queue entries include affected slow suites; pull-request pushes defer them unless requested, and schedules select their declared suites."
"A dedicated weekly flakiness run repeats unit checks even after they pass, and publishes its results."
"An unreadable or empty change base selects all checks available for that event. A push to main whose head lacks a passing merge-queue run starts all merge-queue checks on main."
"A change's run executes each selected check in every configuration that can change its result, and in no other. Within that run, a check's executions in one configuration never overlap, and a pass prevents another start there."
"Browser lanes build each bundle and server once per run; jobs needing those artifacts take the copies already built. Each package run builds each shared test binary and migrates its database template at most once; checks reuse prepared dependencies and shared fixtures."
"CI runs independent browser tests on more than one worker; independent daemon race tests also run concurrently."
"Build and release cancels a check run that a newer run for the same change replaces. Cancellation or coalescing never leaves a merged commit without its affected merge-queue checks."
"The three systems keep each stated size bound of an input, output, stored record or file, with the exceptions that the bound states. An input that a bound refuses gets a reason naming the bound, and a cut output says that it was cut."
"Dispatch counts a session's own messages, comments and asks on an issue since a person last acted there, and advises it at three and six. At six, the advice tells the session to stop posting until a person replies, and a failure drops the whole advice."
"Each operation, background work and event delivery included, serves the load and data size that its bound names, and meets its stated time there. It meets that time at the named share of runs, and a Dispatch page stays within its stated count of requests and size of payload."
"Each check suite, job, step and test keeps its stated bounds of time, machine time, memory, disk and count, by the measure each bound names. A test or step past its time limit fails with a reason naming it, and no check leaves a suite to meet a bound unrecorded."
"Each wait that has a stated time limit stops at that limit and gives a reason that names the limit and what it waited for. The work it stops ends within a stated grace time after the limit, and a hold that outlasts the limit is reported with its cause."
"At a stated bound, a system does what the bound states, keeps answering, and gives a reason for each refusal. It never drops accepted data or work, or shows a partial or stale result as whole, without saying so."
"When pacing is on, Legion holds session wakes in any minute to its configured rate plus burst, and delays a wake rather than dropping it. Each rate or spend limit holds across all replicas of a system, and a caller that one holds back is told so."
"Each operation keeps its stated memory, disk and service bounds, with their stated exceptions, and a refusal at one names it. At its memory budget, Dispatch first closes unused documents; if that frees too little, it refuses new browsers and browser inserts with a shown reason."
"Each stated limit is declared once, and each part that applies, reports or depends on it reads that one declaration. Where one limit must stay within another, a check fails when it does not."
"Legion warns running agents before a planned replacement and states which work and processes the replacement can lose. Before the next turn, Legion reports interruptions, known causes, losses and rebuilt workspaces; conversation loss reports reach the issue, issue owner, controller and new conversation."
"Legion runs each piece of work under exactly one recorded owner: the agent that answers for it, including work shared by change requests. One owner can own several pieces of work, and a statement has no owner."
"When unclosed work leaves its workflow, Legion releases its issue's Dispatch claim held by that work's sessions, even after daemon restarts, leaving other holders unchanged. Dispatch refuses another session's take or release while the holder is live, or unresponsive with a heartbeat at most 20 minutes old."
"Legion saves running agents' conversations outside machines it can replace; recorded external machine terminations and lockouts by older processes consume no budget. After unexpected stops or planned replacements, Legion resumes each agent in its saved conversation, where present, within its death, launch and prompt budgets."
"Legion keeps unfinished tasks across agent and daemon restarts and gives agents in resumed or fresh conversations their owed tasks within all claim budgets. Legion records finished tasks separately and reminds a resumed workflow worker once at registration when its own step remains open with no task to resend."
"Legion detects stalls from recorded activity and deadlines, records recovery steps, and tries the least disruptive recovery without interrupting calls within their allowed time. If recorded inactivity exceeds a running agent's backstop bound, Legion ends its turn and resumes its saved conversation, where present, within all claim budgets."
"When its deployment supplies a fallback chain, Legion follows it in the same conversation after filter refusals, returning to the original model after increasing cooldowns. Provider errors never retire tasks; for errors that can clear, Legion prompts the same conversation again after a growing pause without an attempt limit."
"Legion preserves wakes through release and resumes a released agent's conversation when a message, event or scheduled reminder makes work due. A daemon restart alone resumes no released agent; an explicit stop lasts until a recorded workflow start, which resumes the kept conversation."
"If the store lacks the recorded conversation, Legion starts fresh, uncharged, and reports the loss; if the store cannot answer, Legion waits and retries. Legion compacts conversations between turns on operator or project controller request, keeping owed work; no attempt count makes recovery require store edits."
"Priorities are ranked goals with owners, not issues, and belong to no project; Dispatch lets the person add, rename, reorder and retire them. Each priority's page shows its work and activity, and lets the person edit its owner and description of any length."
"Dispatch lists priorities in rank order with work counts by state, recent completions, open questions and latest activity. Two fault lists show active work without a priority and priority work left in the backlog or set aside."
"Dispatch's server, not its client, refuses board moves naming missing neighbours, another project's neighbours, the issue itself or reversed neighbours, and reports why. It records each project's order, shows it on the board and in the issue table, and marks the issue chosen to start next."
"Dispatch lets the person link work from any project to the same priority, and remove those links. Each end shows its links; lists filter by the priorities advanced or none, and a missing priority never prevents issue creation."
"Dispatch lets the person search issues, filter and sort by project, state, date and issue grade (zero to three), and group by state. Every grouping field has a sortable, filterable column; grade comes first, a bar above counts issues by state, and long lists have pages."
"Dispatch offers saved views of one issue table, with optional Legion details and workflow groups ordered by the workflow without repeating issues. Issue state stays separate from workflow steps; the person sets the date filter on completed work with no fixed age limit."
"Every Dispatch page shows the sum of the two priority fault counts, each counted whole. When no priority exists, it shows no number rather than zero."
"The issue table and Inbox retain all filters, sorts, groupings, counts, views, actions, shortcuts and preferences from 12 November 2026, except what the design replaces. Clearing filters keeps search text, sort, grouping and view; saved addresses restore these choices and filters for retained features."
"Dispatch's single Inbox page lets the person search, filter, sort and group entries by grade (zero to three), urgency, type, project and assignee, including unassigned. Without an address filter, it shows the reader's work; its address restores choices, and it offers time sorting and collapsible groups."
"Dispatch shows what each finished piece of work cost in agent hours, tokens, and money for models and machines. For a sample of that work, it shows recorded estimates of the same work beside that cost."
"Dispatch computes and shows code survival and post-merge rework from the stored patches of every commit on a repository's main branch."
"Every measure that Dispatch shows names its question, unit and owner, and links to the text that states its method. One registry holds every measure of a project's page, with its target and its state."
"Dispatch accepts a reading of a measure from an agent, job or service in one request with its token. It reads every measure from its own database, and never changes or deletes a reading."
"Dispatch shows the lead time of each project's changes to production. For work that Legion runs, it shows work time per step and role, rework, and the wait for capacity, which no work time includes."
"Legion records each move of a piece of work with a time from a machine clock, and counts each backward move by its kind. Dispatch measures work time from these records."
"Dispatch records each deploy and failed change that a system or person reports, and shows how often changes fail and how long recovery takes. A failure counts when its signal arrives, and a rejected one leaves the count but stays on record with its reason."
"A measure that lacks its input for a time, or that Dispatch cannot compute, shows \"no data\" and the reason, never zero. It names the day its input began, and an average or share leaves out each item with no value."
"Each project has its own page of measures, and one read gives the measures of several projects. Each number opens the rows it counts, and each chart compares its window with an earlier one."
"Legion starts new work only on an issue that its project marked for Legion and that Dispatch shows ready for work. Once Dispatch shows the issue done or set aside, Legion starts no new work on it."
"Legion starts the issues that have not started in the order that their project sets in Dispatch. When a place is free, Legion gives it to the first issue in its line that may start, with no act of the controller."
"Work that comes back to an issue that Legion started goes ahead of every issue that has not started. A waiting issue's later start replaces its earlier one in the line, and Legion refuses the earlier assignment's completion."
"Each started issue takes one place under its project's cap, covering the issue, its owner and its work units, not its steps or workers. Legion starts no issue that needs a place while the cap has no room."
"An issue keeps its place while it waits for the person. It gives the place back when it is done or out of the workflow, and while it waits only for its merge."
"An issue that holds a place starts its next step whatever the cap says. Giving a place back stops no agent, and the issue takes one again only when its recorded state requires one."
"Legion reports each issue that it runs as at work, as waiting with its position and its reason, or as held with its reason. It tells the controller of each blocked or held issue."
"Legion starts an issue only when each issue that Dispatch shows blocking it is done, and a blocker in any other state still blocks. A blocked issue keeps its position in the line, and issues behind it may start."
"An issue waiting for Legion to catch up with Dispatch starts nothing and keeps its position, and no issue behind it takes a place. Issues ahead of it still start."
"Legion starts a production check's next look at its work-chosen time, unless the check's stated bounds move or replace that look. Each wake starts one look, repeated reports start no extra look, and a wake during a look starts the next."
"Where a source publishes events for records a system serves, the system learns their changes from those events or reads those events start."
"Reads of event-publishing sources apply the same rules as their events. Neither a read nor a late event replaces a fact already known to be newer."
"A system reads each needed record's source on stated timers or at work-chosen times, unless that record has source events and the system serves it. With Envoy's GitHub notification reader enabled, this includes participating repositories without the Envoy GitHub App."
"Dispatch and Legion publish recorded changes asynchronously, without periodically searching for new entries, and retry failed publications when their retry is due. Dispatch preserves each issue's event order through retries and restarts."
"Envoy gives each supported GitHub change the same event content and recipients, whether GitHub sends it or Envoy reads it. It extracts Slack text in a fixed field order without changing other content, topics or receiving sessions."
"Dispatch refreshes each stored answer and open view after each change that can affect it, and after no other change. An open view runs one read per answer at a time and keeps changes that arrive during that read."
"The systems retry failed GitHub deliveries while GitHub keeps them, and record why recovery ends. Legion resumes a missing event reader after its last applied event rather than at newly arriving events."
"Dispatch rereads checks of served open pull requests every five minutes and records completed head runs within one pass, even when their event was lost. Envoy rereads claims at most two minutes apart and retries incomplete directory bucket reads until complete, while Legion rereads waited-on issues each wake interval."
"Running clients restore registration and the same subscriptions within three minutes after Envoy and NATS recover, or six minutes from failure when both kept answering. While running and scheduled thereafter, sessions read live at every Envoy session-list read in the rig; recovery never revives a cleanly stopped session."
"Between different sessions, Envoy refuses soft takeover when both registrations identify processes and the predecessor's live registration names another process; the refusal identifies the holder. A failed read of the predecessor's registration changes nothing."
"Envoy gives each session one state: gone if stopped cleanly, unregistered or silent over an hour; otherwise live through five minutes since registration, then unresponsive. It keeps unresponsive registrations through service outages and restarts, and a new registration makes the session live at once."
"Envoy removes an expired registration only if it has not changed since the expiry decision. A deregistration naming a process preserves a registration bearing a different process identity."
"Each session client names its own process on registrations, deregistrations, role claims and releases; Envoy records the claimant's process as the role holder. A release frees only its sender's claim; no clean stop or deregistration frees another live process's claim, even within the same session."
"Lookups, message publication, delivery attempts and Legion probes never release an Envoy role claim. Envoy ends a claim only on its holding process's release, a permitted takeover, or a finding that the process named by the claim is gone."
"After a refused role claim, the session client keeps the session registered and running without that role. An Oh My Pi client retries only after a session event, and a Claude Code client only after an explicit claim."
"Each session client subscribes to its own presence check, answers requests there, and advertises the check in its registration only while that subscription is accepted."
"Envoy distinguishes sessions known within thirty days from sessions registered now. A live-session or role-holder lookup never treats that history as a current registration."
"A side that cannot read a message from the other refuses it and says why. A worker plugin's refusal names both contracts, each wrong field, and whether its update round cures the mismatch."
"A service of the three systems and each of its clients follow one written contract for what they exchange. It states each field's shape and meaning, and neither side keeps a second definition."
"Dispatch's clients take the types of its routes and event payloads from one definition, and no side writes a response shape by hand. A client that does not follow a changed route fails to build."
"Envoy's listener and its client test against one shared example of a session row. The listener's test fails where the row it writes differs from what the example states."
"A service of the three systems answers a client of an older contract that it still supports in the shape that contract describes. Legion's daemon so answers each worker plugin of an older supported build, which then reads the body that it read before."
"While two Dispatch builds serve during a release, each accepts the other's requests, stored documents, bus messages and subscription settings. The earlier build still reads and writes the database, and its open pages get no error."
"Legion's daemon and its worker plugins declare one contract number, and a change to what they exchange raises it above its base's. A required check refuses a change that touches the number without raising it, and names the lowest free number."
"Legion's daemon starts only where its configured worker image and its installed plugins speak its own contract number. Where one speaks another number, the daemon refuses to start and says which."
"Legion writes no result of a check that the repository's host counts toward a merge."
"Legion asks the repository's host to merge a change only at the exact head that it accepted for the merge. Legion leaves no request to merge open for a head whose required check failed."
"Legion's own merge conditions, such as a project's hold on some paths or its rule to merge by hand, only add to the repository's rules. Legion shows each condition that holds a change back, with its reason."
"Legion waits for each review that the repository's rules require, and no step of a workflow lifts or dismisses a blocking review. A reviewer that blocks a change takes an answer from the change's author about what the review missed."
"On each repository of the three systems that deploys to production or holds customer data, the host enforces rules on the main branch. They require a pull request, the checks they name and another reviewer's approval of the last push, in force before anything relies on them."
"On each repository of the three systems, a required check passes only when every job that the change's files select has run and passed. A skipped job or an edit of the pull request never turns it green."
"Legion brings each change into its target branch only through a pull request, and through the branch's merge queue where it has one. No identity that Legion uses is exempt from a repository's rules, and Legion changes none of them; the deployer sets them on the repository."
"Legion writes no text of a merge commit, so the repository's own settings decide the message of each merge."
"Legion holds no logic that judges whether the checks of a change allow it to merge. It takes that answer from the repository's host, which applies the repository's rules."
"A statement, component or interface enters, changes or leaves the approved Spec only through the person's approval of a change request. Dispatch refuses that approval from a caller without the person's grant to approve."
"A fix that changes no statement, component or interface, and weakens no check, needs no approval of The Spec, whichever statement it restores. A review examines each added or changed check, and a check left weaker waits for the person's approval."
"Dispatch records the exact text of each approval, a node of The Spec with its checks or a document's version, and reads it back unchanged. It shows that text as approved from that moment; where The Spec is kept in repository files, they hold only approved text."
"Dispatch records each change to approved text after its approval, even one that restores the approved text. It carries the approval to a new version only when each counted answer in it came from a caller with the grant to approve."
"When a write or a reply moves an approval or an open approval request, its answer names each document that moved, with both versions. Pages mark at word level the text changed since a person last approved it."
"The Spec is one tree of statements; each has one parent unless at the top, and an id never changed or reused. A statement states the software's behavior, structure or properties, never an agent's conduct, and names no file, package or symbol."
"Each component and interface is a node of The Spec that the person approves, with a lasting id, a name and a one-sentence purpose. A statement about how the software is built names the components or interfaces that it is about."
"Dispatch holds, for each issue, the document that each of its gated steps waits on, as Legion publishes it. A write that supersedes such an approval names each gate on that document whose issue is not done."
"Legion starts and merges no work that needs an approval, such as work on a changed statement, while that approval does not stand. An approval stands for the version it names until a changes request, or a new version that it was not carried to."
"The command bar answers a typed question in prose from the records of Dispatch, and links the records that the answer rests on. It never links a record that it did not read, and says when an answer rests on no record."
"An answer of the command bar is prose and links by default, and any display it adds is of a closed list of kinds. Each number in a display, such as a chart, comes from a read of Dispatch, never from the model."
"Dispatch's server calls the command bar's models and runs their tools itself, so an answer or operation needs no agent session or Legion daemon. An answer about agents reads the copy of their sessions that Dispatch stores, and says how fresh that copy is."
"Dispatch's server calls the command bar's models through the shared model loop, and holds no model loop of its own. The deployment sets each model, and the bar says what it cannot do while one is unset."
"The command bar runs each write of its catalogue that typed words name, as the person who typed them, and attempts no other named operation. The catalogue holds each write that Dispatch describes as a tool, among them a change to an issue's fields."
"On submit, the command bar runs an operation only when its choice reaches the confidence threshold measured for its model, or the person picks it. It shows each change with the value before and after, and one key reverses, as that person, each change that Dispatch can reverse."
"Each use of the command bar makes a record of its words, what it found, and each write with the value before and after. Only the person who made it reads that record."
"A person opens the command bar from any page with a keyboard shortcut. The bar names the record that it will act on, taken from the page where it opened."
"Dispatch search finds a record by its key, its words or its meaning, and each hit names the record and block that matched. Dispatch says which kinds of record search does not cover."
"Legion delivers no change that leaves a requirement of its approved documents unmet, and no kind of requirement is exempt. Legion's review checks each change against every such requirement before delivery."
"Each behavior that a change adds or fixes has a test that passes at the change's head and fails without the change. For a change that a Dispatch reader sees, that test runs in a browser."
"Each behavior asserted before a change stays asserted by a test that fails when it breaks, unless an approved requirement removes that behavior. The pull request names each removed or changed test, with what now carries its assertion or the approved requirement that removed its subject."
"At a change's head, each text that the change adds or edits is true, and so is each current text that states something it alters. Legion's review passes no change that leaves such a text false; the licence notices of what the three systems ship are current texts."
"Where a change sets a rule for how a repository's code or tests are written or run, the repository's guide for authors states that rule. Each command that such a guide gives works as written at the change's head."
"Each record page presents its activity in the same place relative to the record, rather than choosing a layout for each kind."
"Within each view kind, Dispatch shows each record kind the same way across pages; each field keeps its display format and appearance. For the same record version, field names, values and thread order agree across views."
"Resolved references keep author-written link words; otherwise they show the target's current name, or its text for comments and messages. Missing or unreadable targets leave the written words without a link."
"One shared framework reads, draws and opens each reference form that Dispatch resolves, in every view and editor, including bare issue keys and page addresses. Every reader finds the same references and targets in the same record text, before and after a save."
"Text fields of one writing purpose share one component on every page and record kind, except document bodies and live agent conversations. In-place editors share one box, and every prose editor shares the reference picker and keeps picked references in drafts."
"Every Dispatch table uses a shared table component; no page draws table markup of its own. Lists showing the same kinds of records use the same component, regardless of page."
"Search and sorting controls share components by purpose across pages; filters for the same value type share one component. Column filters open from their headers, and every option list offers search."
"A reply or message whose whole text is one reference shows the same title-and-snippet card in the Inbox, Conversation and margin. A reply or message with any other text shows no such card."
"The conversation view comes first among the issue's views and draws the issue's discussion, each thread with its replies. It draws no document and no event line."
"Each issue has its own page, which shows the issue's conversation, spec, verification, plan and decisions as five views. The page shows one view at a time, and one press moves the reader to any other."
"The issue's page lists every document and attachment of the issue. Opening a document there draws it on the page with its editor, and from its own toolbar the person reads each earlier version still kept."
"From the issue's page, the person reads every recorded event of the issue, such as a status change, a claim or a handoff. That history also opens from an address of its own."
"Wherever the issue's page draws a proposed node, it marks the node Proposed and offers the person a control to settle it. Overtaken nodes fold away behind one line that counts them."
"The issue's page draws each lens, a review of one node, under that node with its kind, author, time and verdict, and its findings beneath. A risk that a pre-mortem found shows its early warning and the check that prevents it, or says that none does."
"Dispatch stores issues, documents, conversations, decisions and everything else in one graph of linked records; new issue documents link to their issue before holding text. Dispatch imports components from several repositories per project and lets an issue link to components from several sources in one update."
"Dispatch stores each relation in the graph with its name, never only as a value of a record. It appends changes to relations and values, keeping authors, times, causes and history unless a permitted deletion removes them."
"Each issue arranges its records in one tree, with one parent per record below its root. Coverage and dependencies are separate relations, so one check or work unit can serve several statements without a copy."
"Dispatch keeps each decision as one record of its original words, linked to its server-recorded author and the question it settles, where one exists. Every display draws from that record without copying or restating the decision, and an agent's reading stays separate."
"Each statement shows the decisions that shaped it, and the reader can follow its checks, work units and results through the recorded relations. Dispatch shows missing links instead of presenting an incomplete chain as proof."
"Dispatch links each reply to the passage or conversation record it answers, and its conversation views show those nested exchanges. Each filtered view shows only the kinds of conversation records it selects."
"Each record keeps its address when its title or words change, and each passage also when its place changes. A reference can follow current text or keep a cited version and show whether that text changed or disappeared."
"Every record has one address in a shared grammar that every Dispatch reader interprets alike; references can name any record across its project. Dispatch resolves references, records backlinks, lists all matches for ambiguous addresses, and keeps unresolved references as written rather than choosing another target."
"Dispatch records mentions as references in the text and offers issue and person references while the person writes. Reference recognition leaves stored text unchanged."
"Every Dispatch page lets the reader navigate its content and use every action with the keyboard alone. Information never depends on colour alone or requires hovering."
"At phone and wider widths, readers can read all content and use every action; phone users need only touch, without hover or hardware keyboards. Only code, tables and pictures scroll sideways; tapping a link's text follows that link, and tapping beside it follows none."
"Others' additions and background data updates keep the reader's text in place while that text and its version remain on screen. Pictures loading and folds opening preserve that place; redraws keep disclosures open, and an open Inbox item keeps its document version."
"Dispatch follows new session text only while the reader is at its newest text; returning or sending to that session resumes following. One control opens the newest session or issue text; reconnecting restores the surviving turn being read to its previous screen position."
"A link or selection opens its target; questions show their document if they have one, and replies show their whole thread and text. Replies sit under their entries; folded replies stay reachable, unread conversations are marked, and sending an issue comment brings it into view."
"Dispatch renders documents without a virtual DOM or virtualized list, skipping offscreen top-level blocks without removing them. An issue document's first screen comes from the server and appears before the application starts."
"An issue header's controls and Inbox action buttons keep their positions through edits, changed values and long titles. Selection bars and controls for new content move neither the list nor the reader's text."
"Dispatch renders text and media in documents, conversations and earlier document versions, with safe links, picture enlargement and navigation, and in-place video playback."
"Dispatch uses plain words and legible control text, without long explanatory prose about the application. Each issue page presents its conversation as an outline, shows its summary before the outline and keeps every property reachable."
"Each question names one signed-in recipient: its handed-on person, else its issue's assignee where assigned; every project names a signed-in default. Every read names that person; Dispatch accepts hand-on only from a person, and open Inboxes follow recipient changes without reload."
"Each wait reaches the one Inbox of every person it waits on; a credential request open to several approvers reaches each approver's Inbox. Those Inboxes include questions, approvals and threads, including issue-less messages, broadcasts and replies; Dispatch lets each person's agent read their Inbox."
"The person's answer or refusal reaches the asking agent; Dispatch sends outcomes and comments through Envoy to followers and claimants and lists answers and declines. Legion resumes the waiting issue owner with the full answer or decline, including the person's note, even when it arrives before Legion records the wait."
"Reading never removes an item; removal requires someone's answer, decline, resolution, withdrawal, thread dismissal or recipient change, not expiry alone. A new message from another person or agent restores a dismissed thread unread; Dispatch accepts comments and messages on closed issues."
"Dispatch distinguishes an answer from a decline, a resolution or a withdrawal, and refuses to decline an approval request. Closing an issue withdraws its open questions, and reopening restores only those questions that the close withdrew."
"The Inbox shows the whole request and conversation, and opens a document's question or comment in that document. The person can answer there, approve or request changes with one key press, and see whether the issue's approval stands."
"Waiting counts mean the same whatever filter the Inbox shows. Each row shows its kind, priority and urgency without hiding its title or issue key, and never stacks badges."
"Dispatch refuses a decision block in a comment or message before storage, and says to use a question or document instead. An existing block in such text explains that the person cannot answer it there."
"Dispatch sends one phone notification per question or approval request waiting on the person, only to phones where that person opted in. It sends no other phone notification; the broker adds no push notification of its own for credential requests, which Dispatch lists."
"Legion checks each read and write that a caller asks of it against that caller's own grants, and refuses what they do not allow. A caller's grants hold even where several callers share one account."
"Where one of the three systems checks who its caller is, the caller proves it with a credential issued to that caller alone. No caller proves its identity with a token that other callers share, or with the credential of another caller."
"Each commit that Legion makes names its caller, and a commit by an agent names its session and role. Each model call that Legion makes names the work behind it."
"Dispatch checks each request against the grants of its credential and the visibility of each record, and refuses what they do not allow. A record that the caller may not read is absent from each page, list, count and search result."
"Envoy's message hub gives each user only the message subjects that its work needs, and refuses every other subscription or publish. Envoy's listeners and secrets broker refuse each request that the caller's identity does not allow."
"Each account that runs or calls the three systems holds only the access its work needs, and every system or platform refuses it the rest. No Legion project can write the session records of another project."
"Dispatch records each act with the person, agent session or service that made it. Where an agent acted for a person, the record and each view of it name both."
"Each Envoy message names its caller, and names a sending session as its current registration gives it. Each secrets broker call names its caller, and the broker's log holds that caller with its network address."
"The three systems let an agent act for a person only under a credential that the person granted, and within that person's own grants. The person can list and revoke each such credential."
"Envoy tells a session when a message accepted for it disappears before it saves that message in its conversation. This includes expiry or byte removal, whether or not delivery or a restart has occurred."
"Envoy accepts a direct send, role message, topic publish or outside event for a session only after storing it. If storage fails, Envoy refuses that acceptance and tells the sender."
"Envoy gives each session that runs, can reach Envoy and has messages stored for it a path that delivers them. Changing or restarting that path leaves unsaved messages available for delivery."
"Envoy keeps each message accepted for a session until that session saves it in its conversation or a stated age or byte limit removes it. Its byte limit holds the full stated age at ten times measured load, and removes oldest messages first."
"While a session runs and can reach Envoy, Envoy redelivers each unsaved message accepted for it until a stated limit removes it. After a session, listener or hub restarts, the session gets each unsaved message still stored for it."
"Envoy routes direct messages to their addressed sessions and role messages to their holders, and gives each matching subscriber one topic copy. A topic's name does not change how Envoy routes it."
"Envoy reports each send's outcome, the recipient's state and last-seen time, and distinguishes sent messages from queued messages. A stored-topic publish reports how many other sessions match, counts each once, and does not fail merely because none match."
"Each session confirms saving only after it records the message and delivery state in its conversation. A session records each message once, also across redeliveries, restarts, resumes and interrupts, and an interrupt drops no queued message."
"An idle session starts a turn for each message, except startup or inert notices, and busy sessions give each arrival to the model. Interrupts keep queued messages for the next turn; a person's late message appears only as a message."
"A failing check's output shows what it read that made it fail, and says so where it could not read or a bound stopped it."
"Where a check names its run in continuous integration as proof, Dispatch reads its result from that run and refuses a recorded one. A verdict on continuous integration that Dispatch or Legion records comes from the change's own runs, read in full."
"Dispatch shows, apart from each result's verdict, whether its commit belongs to the commits or branch in question and whether the check's words changed since. It names them, says why where it cannot tell, and counts a pass only where its commit belongs and the words are unchanged."
"Dispatch holds each check with what must be true, where it runs, and what proves it. A check is proved by a command or by a named run of continuous integration, and Dispatch refuses both together."
"Each check fails when the behavior it claims breaks, and it exercises that behavior itself, not a text about it or a model of it. A pass claims no more than the check exercised."
"Each test owns its servers, data, ports and environment, and waits for facts; it reads real time only where it measures real elapsed time. Each run names each case that it skipped, quarantined or passed only on a second try."
"Each result of a check names the check, the run that produced it, the whole commit that it tested, its verdict and what it observed. Dispatch shows every result, not only the newest, with who recorded it; a surface that cuts what a result observed marks the cut."
"Dispatch keeps each result that it accepts exactly as reported, and never edits or deletes it. A retraction is a new record that names the result, its reason and its author, and the retracted result stays readable but counts for nothing."
"A check that did not run, was skipped or cancelled, is unfinished, or could not be read gives no pass, and its result says which. A verdict that Dispatch or Legion records over checks or cases passes only when there is at least one and each passed."
"The daemon makes all and only the changes that recorded rules require to work, workers, environments, slots or status, without querying models. The same rules, records, events, service answers and due timers give the same changes and requests to merge or reorder work."
"The daemon follows recorded review and check outcomes instead of deciding what they mean for work. Its offline loop answers as the stopped agent in that agent's conversation, starts no environment, and changes no work state."
"The daemon manages work state, not access to an agent's tools or message routes. It creates no commits."
"The daemon records each assignment and delivers each newly owed task once, only to its assigned role. It refuses a completion that names an earlier assignment."
"After a connection failure prevents a claim write, the daemon keeps that write owed while it runs. It retries with the current claim and keeps taking later events."
"The daemon counts work only as recorded limit rules specify, and every failure charge follows one shared rule over observed outcomes. For each registration it refuses, the daemon remembers the generation and charges it no launch attempt until that generation or the daemon process ends."
"Legion reminds only step workers with environments after every turn with their step open, never controllers or helpers, unless declared waits or holds suppress reminders. Reminders ignore reply prose, the first continues its turn, later ones start turns, and Legion sends due notices with unchanged supplied words and event facts."
"The daemon moves work by the pull request and merge state that GitHub reports. It keeps merge submissions while the controller's role holder is unresponsive."
"The daemon combines GitHub and Dispatch events into queryable work state, distinguishing waits, progress, maintenance and environment releases. Its state answers never report work stalled while any of its workers is at work."
"Each agent session connected to Envoy, Legion's included, gives Dispatch each part of its conversation that its program holds: each message, thought and tool call. Where a session cannot give a part or take a message, command or switch, its live view says which and why."
"For a session that reports its models and thinking levels, a person can switch either from its live view, for that session alone. The conversation records each switch and who made it, and the view shows why a switch was refused or that no answer came."
"Where a session's terminal takes slash or exclamation commands, a person's message to that session that starts with such a mark runs as typed there. Its output comes back as the session's reply; a side question or a broadcast runs no command."
"Dispatch lists each agent session that Envoy reports as live, under the session's current name. It never shows part of that list as the whole list."
"Wherever Dispatch names an agent session, it shows whether that session is online, and a click opens the session's live view."
"A session's live view shows the conversation since its process started, within a stated size, then each new part as the agent writes it. Watching the view adds nothing to what the agent reads."
"A session's live view shows the model, thinking level and share of the context window that the session reports. It shows each change without a reload, also between turns."
"Dispatch sends a person's message to a session through Envoy at once, also while the session's turn runs. A session that takes messages joins all but side questions to its running turn at the next tool call, or starts a turn."
"Only a message that Dispatch confirms as a signed-in person's runs a command or a switch, or reaches the session as that person's own. Every other message shows as a card naming its sender, and a session's exclamation commands are open to any signed-in person, not only its owner."
"A document edit preserves words, structure, image links and HTML comments outside its request, and stored replacements read back as written. An image link does not cause a write refusal."
"An edit changes only its named target, its event, questions that it opens or closes, and approval requests that its new document version moves. A refused edit changes no stored content and gives its reason."
"Unchanged blocks in place retain their unique identities unless a question or anchor prevents it, and document edits never remove open questions. Document edits keep discussions on surviving blocks and change only named questions or open approval requests for documents whose new version they write."
"A write preserves concurrent changes or refuses without changing the record. A read that supplies edit conditions gives the content, block identities and conditions from one document version."
"Dispatch shares simultaneous edits, cursors and presence across all open copies and saves accepted text as readable versions, even after issue closure. A closed issue's documents refuse new text from a person or session."
"Dispatch keeps the newest unsaved field choice through refreshes, navigation and reloads, and prevents an older save from overwriting a newer choice. A refused save shows its reason and restores the confirmed value, except that a refused title keeps its draft."
"Dispatch keeps earlier versions of records and attachments readable unless a permitted post deletion or untouched issue document removal hides them. Readers compare document versions in one shared view and see which posts changed and when."
"Dispatch allows only authors to delete comments or issue messages, hiding their current and earlier bodies but keeping replies, position, recorded events and completed deliveries. Dispatch lets a person remove only an issue document with one empty version and no comment, question, result or approval."
"A batch of named edits to document blocks returns a report of the version it wrote. The report covers additions, removals and moves, not a later read."
"With each new issue or question, Dispatch offers the writer the nearest found issues and answered questions, up to a stated number. Below that number it leaves out no found item; for a new issue, open issues come first."
"Unless forced, Dispatch refuses a new issue that nearly copies an open issue of its project, other than an issue whose work it belongs to. A create whose nearest such issue is close, but not a near copy, names that issue as a likely duplicate."
"When someone opens a question on an issue that holds a question the person declined, Dispatch names each declined question apart from the related items. Neither a cap on the items nor a search that was not made removes it."
"Dispatch runs the related-item check on each new issue and each new question, including a question written into a document. Where a person drafts an issue, Dispatch shows the nearest existing issues before it is filed."
"The related-item check finds the issues and answered questions nearest the new text, by meaning and by its rarest words. A refresh of the word counts that it ranks by loses no change that a write queued."
"For a new question, the related-item check offers answered questions of other projects, after the items of its own project and never in their place. Each question offered shows whether it is answered and, if so, who answered, when and the answer."
"Each check that Dispatch runs on a new issue or question reports one of three outcomes: items found, searched and found nothing, or not searched. Not searched never reads like found nothing, and a search by words alone says so."
"The related-item check runs after the issue or question is written, and the writer gets its result with the write, within a stated time. A check that fails or runs out of time never fails or undoes that write."
"Dispatch records the outcome and duration of each related-item check, and each issue forced past a refusal or an unanswered near-copy check. Except on a question written as a block of a document, it also records each offered item and what the filer did with it."
"Dispatch's server answers only requests of one described set, and its pages send no other. A check fails when the server answers a request that is not in the set."
"Each read or change of a record that Dispatch's pages offer has a described request that does the same. The request allows exactly the callers whom the page allows, and needs no open page."
"A terminal command acts by sending requests of that described set with its caller's own credential. The server, and not the command, decides whether the caller may act."
"The command line takes each command and its options from the agent tool contract, with no edit of its own. A check names each request that changes records and that no tool makes."
"Dispatch refuses no request because its caller is not a browser. A credential that the person gave all the powers of their browser may send each request that the browser may send."
"A terminal command places an issue above, below or between named neighbours on its project's board, and its output names the move. A command adds and removes the priorities that an issue advances."
"A terminal command makes a document on an issue from a title alone, and prints the document's reference. Dispatch lets the author of a comment or an issue message edit or delete it by a request or a command."
"Envoy delivers each message that it accepted for a session to that session, and a stop of the session or a listener loses none. When Envoy removed a message before its session came back, Envoy tells the session that messages may be missing."
"Envoy answers a send to a session with success only after the message stream stores the message. When the stream does not store the message, Envoy answers the sender with an error."
"Envoy stores a send to a live or unresponsive session, whose last registration is at most one hour old by default. Envoy refuses a send to a gone session with an answer that starts with the words no live session and names the session."
"A send to a session whose last registration is 90 seconds old gets 200 with the state unresponsive. Once the session reads gone, the same send gets 404 with the error no live session and the session's id."
"While a listener cannot reach the message bus, it refuses each send with an answer that names the bus. The answer asks the sender to try again, and it comes in less than four seconds, inside the sender's default five-second attempt."
"After a network cut disconnects the listener, a send to a registered session gets 503 and Retry-After 1 in under 4 seconds. Its error starts by saying that the message bus did not answer, and the same send before the cut gets 200."
"When the message stream reaches its byte limit, it refuses a new message, and the sender gets an error. The stream removes no stored message to make space."
"With a 16 KiB limit on a three-node cluster's stream, small publishes get a full-stream refusal before the stream stores 1,024 messages."
"Envoy stores a send on the subject of the session that the send names. Envoy does this also when the listener that takes the send finds the session only in the session store."
"When the session store holds a session that the sending listener's own copy does not hold, a send to it gets 200. The same message arrives on that session's own subject."
"Each session receives its messages only through its own durable consumer on the message stream."
"A session's consumer filters only on the session's own subject and on each topic that the session follows and the stream stores."
"A registration in consumer mode that follows two topics gets the relay subject of the session in its answer. The session's consumer filters on exactly the session's own subject and those two topics."
"The account that sessions use may subscribe to no notification subject except the subjects on which listeners relay to sessions."
"The account that sessions use may subscribe to exactly two subject patterns. They are the relay subject of a session and the control subject of the agent stream."
"A session's consumer keeps each message, and delivers it again, until the session reports that it wrote the message to its transcript."
"A listener acknowledges a message to the stream only when the session answers that it wrote the message. An answer that the session only received the message leaves the message waiting for an acknowledgement."
"After the session answers received for a relayed message, the consumer still holds that message for an acknowledgement. After the session answers done, the consumer holds nothing."
"A session's consumer sets no limit on how often it delivers a message. A message that the session never acknowledges stays open for an acknowledgement, however many acknowledgement waits pass."
"After one delivery and no answer, a message stays open for an acknowledgement through three one-second acknowledgement waits. It stays open after a later message gets its acknowledgement, and a restart of the consumer delivers it again."
"When a session answers no relayed message within the receipt wait, the listener pauses its relay to that session. After the pause, the listener sends again each message that the session did not answer."
"A stand-in session stops answering, the listener pauses its relay and logs the pause once, and the stream stores one more message. Once the session answers again, the unanswered message and the new one reach it within the receipt wait plus two first pauses plus two seconds."
"When a session or its listener stops and starts again, the session gets each unacknowledged message that the stream holds for it."
"A session that stopped answering and registers again gets each message that the stream stored for it while it was away."
"Three messages relayed while the session was away reach the session when it registers again with a new epoch, and nothing else arrives."
"When a listener restarts a session's consumer, the consumer delivers again, in stream order, each message that it delivered and that was not acknowledged."
"The consumer delivers four messages, and only the first and the third get an acknowledgement. A restart of the consumer starts after the first and delivers the second, third and fourth again in stream order, then no more."
"When Envoy removes a session's consumer, it records the last message up to which the session acknowledged each message. The consumer that Envoy creates again starts after that message, so the session gets each later message and no earlier one."
"Envoy records the acknowledged first message as the floor and then removes the consumer, and the new consumer starts at the next message. It delivers the unacknowledged message and the message that the stream stored while the session was away, then no more."
"When a session's listener stops and starts again, it relays each message that the stream stored for the session during the stop. It also relays the message that the session did not finish, and neither delivery needs a new registration from the session."
"A kill stops a listener process while the session holds one message unfinished, and the stream stores three messages while it is down. The restarted listener relays those four messages within 10 seconds, without a new registration, and the consumer then holds nothing for an acknowledgement."
"A session's first consumer starts at the first message that the stream holds on the session's own subject. Thus the session gets a message that the stream stored for it before its first registration."
"A message stored on a session's own subject before the session first registers reaches the session after it registers, once."
"The message server removes a session's consumer after a time without use. That time is longer than the session record's life plus one reaper interval and one sweep interval, the bound of Envoy's own removal."
"For three sets of windows, the derived inactive threshold is above the session record's life plus one reaper interval and one sweep interval. Each result is at least one hour."
"When a session's consumer starts below the first message that the stream still holds, Envoy tells the session that messages may be missing."
"A registration whose resume point lies below the stream's first message gets a gap in its answer. Its consumer starts at the message after the resume point."
